# Competitor-default product decisions (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-08-16): **when a product decision would
have been asked of Phil, do not ask.** Default to what CyberArk,
Delinea, BeyondTrust, HashiCorp Vault, 1Password Business, and
Bitwarden Enterprise do for that scenario. If no competitor signal,
choose the most responsible, assessor-defensible option for a
USG/enterprise privileged-identity product (NIST SP 800-53 AC/AU,
attribution, least surprise for auditors). Record the decision and
the basis in the commit body. Do not leave `open-q-*` hanging.

## The rule

> **Never park a product UX, default, or evidence-visibility decision
> on the operator.** Competitor parity first. Else the most
> responsible cybersecurity default. Write the decision and the
> basis into the commit. Close the `open-q-*` in the same change
> set. US English. No lab leaks in UI.

## Required agent behavior

1. **Do not ask** when the alternative is “should we show X?” /
   “what should the default be?” / leaving `open-q-*` for Phil.
2. **Competitor first** — match the named vault/PAM vendors for
   that scenario (who owns a secret, who approved access, what
   is visible in a list by default).
3. **Else responsible default** — attribution (AU-2/AU-3), least
   privilege, fail closed, auditor-least-surprise. Density or
   chrome compactness is not a reason to hide the accountable
   party.
4. **Record the basis** in the commit body: vendors consulted, or
   the 800-53 / attribution reason when no vendor signal.
5. **Close `open-q-*`** in the same change set. Do not add a new
   parked question.
6. Source identifiers and UI copy stay US English
   ([`enterprise-compliance.mdc`](enterprise-compliance.mdc) §1).
   Never put lab configuration or agent thinking in operator UI
   ([`no-lab-config-in-operator-ui.mdc`](no-lab-config-in-operator-ui.mdc)).
7. **Website copy never names those vendors.** Write "Competitors".
   Record vendor names in the commit body or `docs/dev` only.
   See [`no-competitor-names-in-website.mdc`](no-competitor-names-in-website.mdc).

## Forbidden

- Asking the operator to pick a default the named competitors
  already settled.
- Leaving `open-q-*` in a queue, spec comment, or evidence note
  after the slice that found it.
- Hiding an accountable party (owner, approver, actor) to save
  column width.
- Inventing a fake owner / email-as-identity when the person
  record is missing — bind to the durable principal, or say
  unresolved honestly.

## Worked example

**Wrong:** Secure Share Owner column exists on the API but starts
hidden; spec comment says `open-q-owner` and the agent asks
whether to show it.

**Right:** CyberArk / Delinea / Bitwarden Enterprise / 1Password
Business show who owns a shared secret. Show Owner by default;
Columns may hide it. Commit body records competitor + AU-2/AU-3.

